course: dictionary
lesson: 3303
title: Ethics: right, wrong and fair
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ethics are the ideas people use to decide what is right and wrong. They are a guide for how to act when you are not sure what to do. «An ethics olympiad is a competition where school teams discuss real dilemmas.» A dilemma is a hard choice where every option has a problem, so the team has to think carefully.
explain: The context gives you two clues. Ethics is about thinking and discussing, not just following a list of rules. Teams talk about real dilemmas, so ethics is about working out what is fair and good. The word olympiad tells you it is a contest, so ethics can be a subject people compete in.
explain: Ethics appears in other places too. A doctor follows medical ethics, and a company follows business ethics. A person might say, «That goes against my ethics» when they refuse to do something they believe is wrong. Work ethic is different: it means being willing to work hard.
fact: Ethics means ideas about what is right and wrong, and how people should act.
fact: Ethics can also be a school subject, or part of philosophy, that thinks about fairness and how to be good.
fact: A dilemma is a hard choice where every option has a problem.
fact: A code of ethics is a set of written rules for honest behaviour.
fact: Work ethic means being willing to work hard.
exercise: «An ethics olympiad is a competition where school teams discuss real dilemmas.» What is a dilemma? | A quick, easy answer | A kind of school competition | *A hard choice where every option has a problem | A type of school lunch
exercise: In an ethics lesson, what do pupils weigh up when they discuss a dilemma? | Their favourite colours | *Consequences, rules and kindness | Only the price of things | How fast they can run
exercise: What is a work ethic? | *Being willing to work hard | A type of office chair | Being willing to sleep late | A school timetable
test: What does «a code of ethics» mean? | A list of school dinners | *A set of rules for honest behaviour | A secret password | A map of the city
test: Which phrase means you refuse something because you believe it is wrong? | Work ethic | *Against my ethics | Ethics olympiad | Medical records
exam: Which description of ethics is best? | *Ideas about what is right and wrong, and how to act | The name of a town | A sport played only in summer | A type of food
